    <exercise number=" ">
        <title>Question 2.B</title>
        <statement>
            <p>Consider the function <m>f(x) = e^x + \sin(x)</m></p>
            <ul marker="disc">
                <li><p>Use forward difference to approximate <m>f'(-2)</m> using <m>\Delta x = 0.2, 0.1, 0.05, \text{ and } 0.025</m>. In addition, calculate the absolute error for each step size.</p></li>
                <li><p>Use centered difference to approximate <m>f'(-2)</m> using <m>\Delta x = 0.2, 0.1, 0.05, \text{ and } 0.025</m>. In addition, calculate the absolute error for each step size.</p></li>
                <li><p>Compare the results from the two methods and review how order of convergence is reflected in your results.</p></li>
            </ul>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>From Matlab the error for forward (first column) versus centered difference (second column):</p>
            <console>
                <output><![CDATA[
                0.1079    0.0037
                0.0531    0.0009    
                0.0263    0.0002    
                0.0131    0.0001    
                ]]></output>
            </console>
        </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise number=" ">
        <title>Question 2.C</title>
        <statement>
            <p>Consider the function <m>f(x) = e^x + \sin(x)</m> and use centered difference to approximate <m>f''(-2)</m> using <m>\Delta x = 0.2, 0.1, 0.05, \text{ and } 0.025</m>. In addition, calculate the absolute error for each step size.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>From Matlab the centered difference error is:</p>
            <console>
                <output><![CDATA[
                0.002575233455464
                0.000644678315616
                0.000161223977853
                0.000040309394840
                ]]></output>
            </console>
        </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise number=" ">
        <title>Question 2.D</title>
        <statement>
            <p>Consider the data from Question 1.A. Suppose we take a portion of the cubic spline interpolation from Matlab to approximate the derivative when the depth is -7 meters. This can be accomplished by the following:</p>
            <program language="matlab">
                <input><![CDATA[
                >> x=[-5; -6; -7; -8; -9];
                >> y=[18.3; 18.2; 17.6; 11.7; 9.9];
                >> pp = spline(x,y);
                ]]></input>
            </program>
            <p>This stores all the information in a struct in Matlab. If you just want the coefficients then that can be accessed via</p>
            <program language="matlab">
                <input><![CDATA[
                >> [~, coeffs] = unmkpp(pp);
                ]]></input>
            </program>
            <p>In Matlab, if you type coeffs then the coeffs array list the coefficients for each spline in decreasing order. Thus, the coefficients we are interested in are in the third row of the array with the first element of the third row being the coefficient for <m>(x-(-7))^3</m>, the second element being the coefficient for <m>(x-(-7))^2</m>, the third element being the coefficient for <m>(x-(-7))</m>, and the last entry should be 17.6 to match the data entry for -7. Use the information from the Matlab spline function to use the spline to estimate the derivative at a depth of -7.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>The coeffs array is given by</p>
            <console>
                <output><![CDATA[
                -2.1583    8.5250   -4.5667    9.9000
                -2.1583    2.0500    6.0083   11.7000
                 1.3917   -4.4250    3.6333   17.6000
                 1.3917   -0.2500   -1.0417   18.2000
                ]]></output>
            </console>
            <p>which means the derivative at a depth of -7 is the linear coefficient, 3.6333.</p>
        </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise number=" ">
        <title>Question 2.E</title>
        <statement>
            <p>Would the answer in Question 2.D change if you added more data points? Please be complete in your answer.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>The answer would change slightly since all the data points are in the linear system for cubic splines.</p>
        </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise number=" ">
        <title>Question 2.F</title>
        <statement>
            <p>Recall that the centered difference method (<m>f'(x) \approx \frac{f(x+\Delta x) - f(x-\Delta x)}{2\Delta x}</m>) is second order while forward difference is first order.</p>
            <ul marker="disc">
                <li><p>What is the mathematical definition of a first order method in this context and compare this to the definition of second order.</p></li>
                <li><p>What is the advantage of using a second order method? Please explain as completely as possible and illustrate with an example.</p></li>
                <li><p>What is the advantage of using a first order method?</p></li>
                <li><p>Is it possible to use higher order methods? How can a third or fourth order method be generated? (You can be general in your description of how a higher order method can be generated).</p></li>
            </ul>
        </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise number="">
        <title>Question 2.G</title>
        <statement>
            <p>Take the data in Question 1.A and use the information to approximate the second derivative at a depth of -7 meters. What does the second derivative represent in this example?</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>Centered Difference approximation of the second derivative results in -5.3. This represents the temperature change per meter squared. The analogy would be the <q>acceleration</q> of temperature change relative to change in depth.</p>
        </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise number="">
        <title>Question 2.H</title>
        <statement>
            <p>Let x and y be vectors that contain the x values and the f(x) values for a function respectively. We will assume the values in x are strictly increasing. Develop the MATLAB code to calculate the forward difference for this data set and compare this with the MATLAB code to calculate the backward difference.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>There is only a slight difference between these two calculations. The command central to our work is the <c>diff</c> command. <c>diff(x)</c> results in <c>[x(2)-x(1), x(3)-x(2), ...., x(n)-x(n-1)]</c>. Notice this vector has one less entry than the original vector x. The difference quotient we are looking for is <c>dY = diff(y)./diff(x)</c>. The key detail is the forward difference is comprised of <c>x(1:n-1)</c> and <c>dY</c>, while the backward difference is comprised by <c>x(2:n)</c> and <c>dY</c>. The difference quotient <c>dY</c> is the forward difference calculation for the left endpoint, but it is the backward difference estimate for the right endpoint.</p>
        </solution>
    </exercise>

    <p>
        <alert>Numerical Integration</alert></p>
        <exercise number="">
           <statement> <p>
                Problems from Section 4.4 in [LB16]: 1 a) b) c) d), 2 a) b) c) d), 13
            </p></statement>
    
        <solution>
            <p><em>problem 1 (Midpoint approach / Left Hand variants):</em></p>
            <p>a) <m>\int_1^3 \ln(\sin(x))\, dx</m></p>
            <console>
                <input><![CDATA[f=@(x) log(sin(x)); --> (2/3)*(f(4/3)+f(2)+f(8/3))]]></input>
                <output><![CDATA[ans = -0.604014605941021]]></output>
            </console>
            <p>b) <m>\int_5^7 \sqrt{x\cos(x)}\, dx</m></p>
            <console>
                <input><![CDATA[f=@(x) sqrt(x.*cos(x)); -->(2/3)*(f(16/3)+f(6)+f(20/3))]]></input>
                <output><![CDATA[ans = 4.432124134305341]]></output>
            </console>
            <p>c) <m>\int_1^4 \frac{e^x \ln(x)}{x}\, dx</m></p>
            <console>
                <input><![CDATA[f=@(x) (exp(x).*log(x))./x;--> (f(3/2)+f(5/2)+f(7/2))]]></input>
                <output><![CDATA[ans = 17.529617332483518]]></output>
            </console>
            <p>d) <m>\int_{10}^{13} \sqrt{1+\cos^2(x)}\, dx</m></p>
            <console>
                <input><![CDATA[f=@(x) sqrt(1+(cos(x).^2));--->f(10.5)+f(11.5)+f(12.5)]]></input>
                <output><![CDATA[ans = 3.63063557627380]]></output>
            </console>

            <p><em>problem 1 (Trapezoidal variants):</em></p>
            <p>a) <m>\int_1^3 \ln(\sin(x))\, dx</m></p>
            <console>
                <input><![CDATA[f=@(x) log(sin(x)); -->(1/3)*(f(1)+2*f(5/3)+2*f(7/3)+f(3))]]></input>
                <output><![CDATA[ans = -0.929469389751241]]></output>
            </console>
            <p>b) <m>\int_5^7 \sqrt{x\cos(x)}\, dx</m></p>
            <console>
                <input><![CDATA[f=@(x) sqrt(x.*cos(x)); --> (1/3)*(f(5)+2*f(17/3)+2*f(19/3)+f(7))]]></input>
                <output><![CDATA[ans = 4.272884027016091]]></output>
            </console>
            <p>c) <m>\int_1^4 \frac{e^x \ln(x)}{x}\, dx</m></p>
            <console>
                <input><![CDATA[f=@(x) (exp(x).*log(x))./x;-->.5*(f(1)+2*f(2)+2*f(3)+f(4))]]></input>
                <output><![CDATA[ans = 19.377396036905900]]></output>
            </console>
            <p>d) <m>\int_{10}^{13} \sqrt{1+\cos^2(x)}\, dx</m></p>
            <console>
                <input><![CDATA[f=@(x) sqrt(1+(cos(x).^2));--->0.5*(f(10)+2*f(11)+2*f(12)+f(13))]]></input>
                <output><![CDATA[ans = 3.636350478753207]]></output>
            </console>
        </solution> </exercise>

