    <exercise number="">
        <title>Question 6</title>
        <statement>
            <p>Recall the example in class (that comes from [KK09]), which is a table of velocity values for a rocket:</p>
            <tabular top="major" bottom="major" left="minor" right="minor">
                <row bottom="minor"><cell halign="left">time</cell><cell halign="center">velocity</cell></row>
                <row bottom="major"><cell halign="left">secs</cell><cell halign="center">m/s</cell></row>
                <row bottom="minor"><cell halign="left">0</cell><cell halign="center">0</cell></row>
                <row bottom="minor"><cell halign="left">10</cell><cell halign="center">227.04</cell></row>
                <row bottom="minor"><cell halign="left">15</cell><cell halign="center">362.78</cell></row>
                <row bottom="minor"><cell halign="left">20</cell><cell halign="center">517.35</cell></row>
                <row bottom="minor"><cell halign="left">22.5</cell><cell halign="center">602.97</cell></row>
                <row bottom="minor"><cell halign="left">30</cell><cell halign="center">901.67</cell></row>
            </tabular>
            <p>Estimate the total distance traveled by the rocket based on this data. Please discuss how accurate your estimate may be.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>Using trapezoidal results in approximately 11,852.9 meters. Trapezoidal is second order so this should be more accurate than simple left or right, we cannot use the Simpson's methods since the step size is not uniform. Using Matlab to deploy adaptive integration using the cubic spline results in 11,749.96 meters, which should be more accurate.</p>
        </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise number="">
        <title>Question 7</title>
        <statement>
            <p>In Matlab, one of the adaptive quadrature methods is the quad function. To use quad to estimate <m>\int_0^1 \frac{dx}{\sqrt{1+x^4}}</m>. The Matlab code is:</p>
            <program language="matlab">
                <input><![CDATA[
                >> f = @(x) 1./sqrt(1+x.^4);
                >> quad(f,0,1)
                ]]></input>
            </program>
            <ul marker="disc">
                <li><p>What is the estimate that Matlab quad gives for <m>\int_0^1 \frac{dx}{\sqrt{1+x^4}}</m>?</p></li>
                <li><p>Should this integral exist?</p></li>
                <li><p>How do we know if this estimate is accurate?</p></li>
            </ul>
            <p>It should be noted that quad is a rather old command in Matlab and if you consult the contemporary versions of Matlab then the integral command will likely be the command they will push. The integral command has the syntax:</p>
            <program language="matlab">
                <input><![CDATA[
                >> f = @(x) 1./sqrt(1+x.^4);
                >> integral(f,0,1)
                ]]></input>
            </program>
            <p>One advantage to the use of the integral command is the ability to handle improper integrals</p>
            <program language="matlab">
                <input><![CDATA[
                >> f = @(x) 1./sqrt(1+x.^4);
                >> integral(f,0,Inf)
                ]]></input>
            </program>
            <p>The quad command often struggles with this kind of problem.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <program language="matlab">
                <input><![CDATA[
                >> f = @(x) 1./sqrt(1+x.^4);
                >> integral(f,0,Inf)
                ]]></input>
                <output><![CDATA[
                ans =    1.8541    
                ]]></output>
            </program>
            <p>Thus,</p>
            <me>\int_0^{\infty} \frac{dx}{\sqrt{1+x^4}} \approx 1.8541</me>
        </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise number="">
        <title>Question 8</title>
        <statement>
            <p>Use MATLAB to program a composite Simpson's 1/3 method and use this program to approximate <m>\int_0^1 \frac{dx}{\sqrt{1+x^4}}</m>.</p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise number="">
        <title>Question 9</title>
        <statement>
            <p>Use MATLAB to program a trapezoidal rule where the spacing is not uniform between all the x values. Let the input be vectors x and y that are the x values and y are the corresponding functional values. Please assume x is strictly increasing.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>Here is an example.</p>
            <program language="matlab">
                <input><![CDATA[
                function [flag, I] = trapgen(x, y)
                % [flag, I] = trapgen(x, y)
                % Numerically integrates using the trapezoidal rule
                %         for unequally spaced x-values (assume x is strictly increasing)
                %
                %   Inputs:
                %     x - vector of x-values (not necessarily equally spaced)
                %     y - vector of corresponding y-values
                %
                %   Output:
                %     I - approximate value of the integral
                %     flag = 0 --> output is fine
                %     flag = 1 -->  x and y are not the same length
                %     flag = 2 -->  x does not have at least two points
                I = 0; 

                    % Input check
                    if length(x) ~= length(y)
                        flag = 1;
                    elseif length(x) < 2
                        flag = 2; 
                    end % end if

                    % Loop through each subinterval
                    for i = 1:length(x)-1
                        h = x(i+1) - x(i);
                        I = I + 0.5 * h * (y(i) + y(i+1));
                    end %end for
                end %end function
                ]]></input>
            </program>
        </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise number="">
        <title>Question 10</title>
