            <p>Consider the integral <m>\int_0^1 \frac{\sin(x)}{x} \, dx</m>. Use Matlab to estimate the value of this integral. Should this integral have a finite value? (Notice the function is not defined when x=0.)</p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise number="">
        <title>Question 11</title>
        <statement>
            <p>Table 1 in Chapter 7, Section 5 of [KK09] gives a few points and weights for Gaussian Quadrature. Consider the integral <m>\int_{-1}^1 (e^x + e^{-x}) \, dx</m></p>
            <ul marker="disc">
                <li><p>Use Gaussian Quadrature 2, 3, and 4 point methods to approximate <m>\int_{-1}^1 (e^x + e^{-x}) \, dx</m> and find the absolute error for each approximation.</p></li>
                <li><p>Use Trapezoidal Rule with step sizes of: <m>\Delta x = 0.5, 0.25, \text{ and } 0.2</m> to approximate <m>\int_{-1}^1 (e^x + e^{-x}) \, dx</m> and find the absolute error for each step size.</p></li>
                <li><p>Compare your results from Gaussian Quadrature versus the Trapezoidal Rule.</p></li>
            </ul>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <ul marker="disc">
                <li><p>First part  with two point: <m>f(-\sqrt{3}/3) + f(\sqrt{3}/3) = 4.685392175819461</m> with error = 0.015412598755745. Three point: 4.700673880743191 with error <m>1.3089383201503\times 10^{-4}</m>. Four point: 4.700804184312754 with error <m>5.902624522491351\times 10^{-7}</m>.</p></li>
                <li><p>Second part: <m>\Delta x = 0.5 \rightarrow 4.798332565228005</m> with error 0.097527790652799. <m>\Delta x = 0.25 \rightarrow 4.725262667170421</m> with error 0.024457892595215, <m>\Delta x = 0.2 \rightarrow 4.716463687529812</m> with error 0.015658912954605.</p></li>
                <li><p>Third part: Gaussian Quadrature does very well for just four points it is more accurate than Trapezoidal with 11 points.</p></li>
            </ul>
        </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise number="">
        <title>Question 12</title>
        <statement>
            <p>Gaussian Quadrature is designed to be exact for certain functions. For the 4 point Gaussian Quadrature method, describe the functions for which the method should be exact.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>The rule is that an n point formula is exact for polynomials of degree <m>2n-1</m> or lower.</p>
        </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise number="">
        <title>Question 13</title>
        <statement>
            <p>We discussed earlier how everything we have covered has connections to interpolation. For example, forward and backward difference methods for derivatives is really using linear interpolation, and the Trapezoidal Rule in integration is also the use of linear interpolation. Does Simpson's 1/3 Rule have connection to interpolation and if so what is the connection?</p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise number="">
        <title>Question 14</title>
        <statement>
            <p>Trapezoidal and the Simpson's methods are known as fixed point methods. On the other hand, Gaussian Quadrature and adaptive methods are not fixed point methods. Explain the differences between the fixed point methods and the methods that are not fixed point methods.</p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>    

    <exercise number="">
        <title>Question 15</title>
        <statement>
            <p>True or False? Adaptive Methods are more accurate than fixed point methods. Please explain your answer.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution> <p>Adaptive methods are built for efficiency. Accuracy can be improved ina fixed point method by shrinking the step size, which usually entails higher computational cost. Adaptive methods are built to achieve accuracy, but a lower computational cost. It is possible for an adaptive method to have a higher computational cost since they have a significant start up cost, but this usually reserved for very simple functions. </p> 
            </solution>
    </exercise>
    
</exercises>
